    "topic": "jack-o'-lanterns were originally turnips",
    "summary": "Halloween lanterns in Ireland and Britain were hollowed turnips or mangel wurzels carved with grotesque faces. The name first meant a man with a lantern and the ignis fatuus; carved pumpkins took the name in America by 1837.",
    "angle": "Your pumpkin is standing in for a turnip.",
    "claims": [
      {
        "id": "c1",
        "text": "Turnips or mangel wurzels, hollowed out to act as lanterns and often carved with grotesque faces, were used on Halloween in parts of England, Ireland, Wales and Scotland.",
        "sources": [
          {
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jack-o%27-lantern",
            "title": "Jack-o'-lantern - Wikipedia",
            "quote": "turnips or mangel wurzels, hollowed out to act as lanterns and often carved with grotesque faces"
          }
        ],
        "confidence": "high"
      },
      {
        "id": "c2",
        "text": "The custom of making jack-o'-lanterns at Halloween is believed to have begun in Ireland and Britain.",
        "sources": [
          {
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jack-o%27-lantern",
            "title": "Jack-o'-lantern - Wikipedia",
            "quote": "It is believed that the custom of making jack-o'-lanterns at Halloween time began in Ireland and Britain."
          }
        ],
        "confidence": "high"
      },
      {
        "id": "c3",
        "text": "The term jack-o'-lantern was used from 1663 to 1704 for a man with a lantern or a night watchman, and originally described the ignis fatuus (will-o'-the-wisp).",
        "sources": [
          {
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jack-o%27-lantern",
            "title": "Jack-o'-lantern - Wikipedia",
            "quote": "from 1663 to 1704 in reference to a man with a lantern or to a night watchman"
          }
        ],
        "confidence": "high"
      },
      {
        "id": "c4",
        "text": "A mid-18th-century Irish folk tale tells of Stingy Jack, a lazy but shrewd blacksmith who uses a cross to trap Satan.",
        "sources": [
          {
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jack-o%27-lantern",
            "title": "Jack-o'-lantern - Wikipedia",
            "quote": "An old Irish folk tale from the mid-18th century tells of Stingy Jack, a lazy yet shrewd blacksmith who uses a cross to trap Satan."
          }
        ],
        "confidence": "high"
      },
      {
        "id": "c5",
        "text": "The term is first applied to carved pumpkins in American English in 1837.",
